1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,903
In an ordinary C program, data often sits out in the open, and any function can change it.

2
00:00:06,903 --> 00:00:13,999
One buggy function sets a student's marks to minus five hundred, and every report that reads it is now wrong.

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,837
Object oriented programming seals the data inside an object.

4
00:00:18,837 --> 00:00:23,876
The only way in is through the object's own methods, which can check what they are given.

5
00:00:23,876 --> 00:00:26,172
Minus five hundred is simply rejected.

6
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:37,193
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 5: Object Oriented Programming.

7
00:00:39,567 --> 00:00:40,675
Six parts.

8
00:00:40,675 --> 00:00:42,355
Programming paradigms.

9
00:00:42,355 --> 00:00:44,113
Classes and objects.

10
00:00:44,113 --> 00:00:46,691
Encapsulation and abstraction.

11
00:00:46,691 --> 00:00:47,902
Inheritance.

12
00:00:47,902 --> 00:00:49,165
Polymorphism.

13
00:00:49,165 --> 00:00:52,400
And the advantages and applications of OOP.

14
00:00:56,733 --> 00:01:00,094
A paradigm is a style of organising a program.

15
00:01:00,094 --> 00:01:08,388
Procedural programming is a sequence of instructions grouped into functions, usually sharing global data, designed top down.

16
00:01:08,388 --> 00:01:17,724
Structured programming adds discipline: only sequence, selection and loops, no goto, and modules that talk through parameters.

17
00:01:17,724 --> 00:01:18,935
C is both.

18
00:01:18,935 --> 00:01:29,240
Object oriented programming organises the program around the data instead: objects that bundle data with the functions that act on it, designed bottom up.

19
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:31,302
Compare.

20
00:01:31,302 --> 00:01:39,739
In C, the data and the area function are separate, and nothing stops another part of the program writing a negative length.

21
00:01:39,739 --> 00:01:49,110
In C plus plus, the length and breadth are private inside the class; the only way in is the constructor and the only way out is area.

22
00:01:53,900 --> 00:02:00,230
A class is a user defined type that binds data members and member functions under one name.

23
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:04,045
It is only a blueprint: it uses no memory for data.

24
00:02:04,045 --> 00:02:11,831
An object is an instance, a real variable of that class, created at run time with its own copy of the data.

25
00:02:11,831 --> 00:02:14,821
One Student class, three student objects.

26
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:21,985
A constructor is a member function with the same name as the class and no return type.

27
00:02:21,985 --> 00:02:27,753
It runs automatically when an object is created, to give the data its starting values.

28
00:02:27,753 --> 00:02:31,113
You can have a default constructor and a parameterised one.

29
00:02:31,113 --> 00:02:37,311
The destructor, with a tilde, runs automatically when the object is destroyed.

30
00:02:37,311 --> 00:02:43,768
And a static member has one copy shared by every object: here, a count of students created.

31
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:54,194
Encapsulation wraps data and its functions into one unit, the class.

32
00:02:54,194 --> 00:02:56,316
The result is data hiding.

33
00:02:56,316 --> 00:02:59,975
Private members can be used only inside the class.

34
00:02:59,975 --> 00:03:04,259
Protected members are also visible to derived classes.

35
00:03:04,259 --> 00:03:07,800
Public members form the interface everyone can use.

36
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,802
Outside code reaches only the public part.

37
00:03:12,033 --> 00:03:17,035
Because marks is private, every change goes through a public setter that can check it.

38
00:03:17,035 --> 00:03:20,394
Set a mark of one hundred and five: rejected.

39
00:03:20,394 --> 00:03:24,782
Write to marks directly from outside: the compiler refuses.

40
00:03:24,782 --> 00:03:31,709
Abstraction is the other side: the user sees only what the object does, not how it is stored.

41
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:36,192
Encapsulation is how it is hidden; abstraction is what is shown.

42
00:03:40,967 --> 00:03:48,456
Inheritance lets a derived class acquire everything in a base class, then add or redefine members.

43
00:03:48,456 --> 00:03:54,810
It gives reuse, and expresses an is a relationship: a teacher is a person.

44
00:03:54,810 --> 00:03:58,364
Single inheritance: one base, one derived.

45
00:03:58,364 --> 00:04:00,383
Multilevel: a chain.

46
00:04:00,383 --> 00:04:03,677
Multiple: one class with two bases.

47
00:04:03,677 --> 00:04:07,049
Hierarchical: one base, many derived.

48
00:04:07,049 --> 00:04:08,300
And hybrid mixes them.

49
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:18,714
Create a Teacher object and the Person constructor runs first, then the Teacher constructor: build the foundation before the house.

50
00:04:18,714 --> 00:04:23,441
When the object dies, destructors run in exactly the reverse order.

51
00:04:23,441 --> 00:04:24,962
A favourite one mark question.

52
00:04:29,733 --> 00:04:35,086
Polymorphism means many forms: one name, several behaviours.

53
00:04:35,086 --> 00:04:40,841
With function overloading, several functions share a name but differ in their parameters.

54
00:04:40,841 --> 00:04:49,461
Call volume with three integers, and the compiler, before the program even runs, picks the matching version: sixty.

55
00:04:49,461 --> 00:04:51,380
That is compile time polymorphism.

56
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:54,203
Run time polymorphism.

57
00:04:54,203 --> 00:04:57,120
A base class pointer holds a derived object.

58
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:04,893
Call show, which is not virtual: the compiler binds it early, by the pointer's type, and the base version runs.

59
00:05:04,893 --> 00:05:13,435
Call draw, which is virtual: the choice waits until the program runs, looks at the real object, and the derived version runs.

60
00:05:13,435 --> 00:05:19,173
That late, dynamic binding is what lets one loop treat teachers and students differently.

61
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:22,836
Overloading against overriding.

62
00:05:22,836 --> 00:05:28,657
Overloading is in one class, with different signatures, resolved at compile time.

63
00:05:28,657 --> 00:05:37,784
Overriding redefines a base function in a derived class with the identical signature, resolved at run time, and needs the word virtual.

64
00:05:37,784 --> 00:05:43,721
Objects interact by sending messages: calling a member function on a named object.

65
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:49,895
Why use objects?

66
00:05:49,895 --> 00:05:53,163
Tested classes can be reused and extended.

67
00:05:53,163 --> 00:05:55,403
Private data is safe.

68
00:05:55,403 --> 00:05:58,606
A bug can only be in the class that owns the data.

69
00:05:58,606 --> 00:06:03,150
Objects mirror real things, so designs are easier to discuss.

70
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:07,239
New features often mean a new class, not edits everywhere.

71
00:06:07,239 --> 00:06:09,908
And teams can split the work by class.

72
00:06:09,908 --> 00:06:14,750
The costs: larger, slightly slower programs and more design time.

73
00:06:15,967 --> 00:06:24,653
And where it is used: graphical interfaces, games, simulations, web frameworks, banking and enterprise systems.

74
00:06:24,653 --> 00:06:30,193
For a single formula, though, a short procedural program is still the right tool.

75
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:33,370
Pause and think.

76
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:45,707
The base class constructor runs first; destructors run in the reverse order.

77
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:48,135
One more.

78
00:06:55,767 --> 00:07:01,069
Run time polymorphism: the version is chosen from the real object while the program runs.

79
00:07:03,267 --> 00:07:04,336
Quick recap.

80
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:05,456
Paradigms.

81
00:07:05,456 --> 00:07:09,727
Classes, objects, constructors and destructors.

82
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:12,227
Encapsulation and abstraction.

83
00:07:12,227 --> 00:07:14,349
The five kinds of inheritance.

84
00:07:14,349 --> 00:07:17,548
And polymorphism, at compile time and at run time.

85
00:07:21,733 --> 00:07:24,130
That was object oriented programming.

86
00:07:24,130 --> 00:07:30,185
Next, the software process model: how real software is planned, built and tested.

87
00:07:30,185 --> 00:07:30,815
See you there.

